Symptom: a small subset of documents (one user, one product, one counter) receives most writes. WiredTiger serialises writes per document. The cluster has spare capacity but that one document caps throughput.
Examples:
A "global stats" doc incremented on every event.
A celebrity user's profile updated by every fan's like.
An auto-increment sequence counter.
Fixes:
Sharded counter pattern — split one counter into N shards, increment a random one, sum on read:
// write
const shardId = Math.floor(Math.random() * 16);
db.counters.updateOne(
{ _id: `pageviews:${shardId}` },
{ $inc: { count: 1 } },
{ upsert: true }
);
// read
db.counters.aggregate([
{ $match: { _id: { $regex: "^pageviews:" } } },
{ $group: { _id: null, total: { $sum: "$count" } } }
])
Move to per-event docs — instead of incrementing a counter, insert one doc per event; aggregate on read. Trade write hotspot for storage + aggregation cost. Use TTL to bound storage.
Async aggregation — log raw events; a background job rolls them up periodically (change stream + $merge).

GridFS splits large files into chunks (default 255 KB) stored across two collections (<bucket>.files + <bucket>.chunks).
import { GridFSBucket } from 'mongodb';
const bucket = new GridFSBucket(db, { bucketName: 'uploads' });
fs.createReadStream('big.mp4').pipe(
bucket.openUploadStream('big.mp4', { metadata: { uploader: 'ada' } })
);
bucket.openDownloadStreamByName('big.mp4').pipe(
fs.createWriteStream('downloaded.mp4')
);
Use GridFS when:
Files > 16 MB.
You want metadata + access control alongside the file in one system.
Replication + sharding of the files for free.
Better alternatives (in 2026):
S3 (or other object storage) + store URL + metadata in MongoDB. Cheaper, faster, scales further.
GridFS is convenient for small projects; object storage wins at scale.
